a list aligned with itself pairs, drops and inserts nothing: true
an element replaced between two kept ones pairs with its replacement: true
two adjacent replacements pair in order: true
a replacement beside an insertion pairs nothing: which replaced which is not determined: true
a replacement at either end still pairs: true
a rewording the row pairs one for one leaves nothing unresolved: true
the rewritten row is the new extraction, in order: true
the reworded direct failure's evidence key follows it, its unchanged sibling's stays: true
the reworded control claim's label follows it, an unchanged claim's stays: true
a synced manifest plans nothing more: true
the plan names the row, the re-key and the relabel: true
a golden's replaced markers and reworded claim rows are rewritten, and nothing else: true
a golden holding none of them is unchanged: true
a key whose failure was reworded beside an insertion is unresolved, not guessed: true
a direct failure reworded into a claim leaves its key unresolved: true
a direct failure turned into a claim of the same words leaves its key unresolved: true
a control claim reworded into a format with substitutions leaves its label unresolved: true
a key that answers to no current failure and no rewording is unresolved: true
a control file's reworded claim is paired old to new, its unchanged one is not: true
and a one-for-one rewording leaves no old claim unpaired: true
an evidence label naming a claim reworded outside the scanner sources is unresolved, with the new text as its hint: true
and is not relabeled: true
a control file's claim reworded beside an insertion leaves the label naming it unresolved: true
and relabels nothing: true
a key whose format's two sites were reworded differently is unresolved, as is its marker: true
and no entry is re-keyed to either successor (the row is still rewritten): true
and no golden swaps its marker for either successor: true
a claim reworded at one site while it survives at another is unresolved: true
and no evidence label follows it (the row is still rewritten): true
nor does a control file's rename of it: the label names the surviving site: true
a control file deleted since the base leaves every old claim unpaired: true
an evidence label a reworded formatted claim rendered is unresolved, not left stale: true
and is not rewritten: true
the claims-only pairing alone does pair the reworded claim: true
but the row's alignment outranks it: the evidence label is not relabeled: true
and the label naming it is unresolved: true
two claims that only swap places leave nothing unpaired: true
a claim another scanner source still makes, reworded in one, is unresolved: true
and no evidence label follows it (the row is still rewritten): true
another scanner's evidence naming the reworded label is unresolved, not relabeled: true
and nothing relabels it: true
a golden row of the same label in another source's golden is not rewritten: true
while the reworded claim's own source's golden row is: true
a re-key onto a key another entry holds is unresolved: true
and is not made: true
the text reader reads the real manifest's rows as compiled: true
and its direct-evidence entries: true
and its catalogue-only entries: true
every row's marker list spans a bracketed list of the manifest text: true
a listed direct failure no table classifies and no golden prints needs an entry, alone: true
one a golden of its source prints -- a control observing it elsewhere -- needs none: true
nor does one an evidence or a catalogue-only entry classifies: a synced manifest: true
nor one its row does not list yet: that is the row's sync, not an entry's: true
an offer line is read back as it was written, and no other line is: true
a line carrying an offer only after other text is no offer: offers start their line: true
a label spanning lines is never offered on a line: true
a failure one claim answers for is offered as the entry naming that claim, under one heading, with the argument accepting it: true
several claims, from one run or several, are offered once each, as alternatives: true
a failure no run's claim answers for is named with what settles it, with no entry: true
an offer for a key needing no entry renders nothing: a synced manifest is quiet: true
an accepted offer is the table's last entry, its claim's text verbatim, the rest unchanged: true
two accepted at once land in the order given, into an empty table too: true
an offer for no row's source, for no current direct failure, for a catalogue-only key, an entry already held or given twice, or an empty claim, is refused, naming why: true
